# Security Verification for Git Commits
# This file documents what is protected from being committed

# ✅ PROTECTED FILES (will NOT be committed):
#
# 1. Environment files with secrets:
#    - .env (all variants)
#    - .env.*
#    - *.env
#    - env.backup
#
# 2. Database files:
#    - *.db
#    - *.sqlite
#    - *.duckdb
#
# 3. Encryption keys:
#    - db/strategy_encryption.key
#    - keys/ (OAuth keys for Remote MCP)
#
# 4. Logs and temporary files:
#    - *.log
#    - *.tmp
#    - log/
#    - tmp/
#
# 5. Workspace (personal strategies):
#    - workspace/**
#
# 6. Build artifacts:
#    - node_modules/
#    - dist/
#    - build/
#    - .venv/

# ✅ VERIFICATION COMMANDS:
#
# Check if .env is ignored:
#   git check-ignore .env
#
# Check what would be committed:
#   git status
#   git diff --cached
#
# Search for potential secrets before commit:
#   git diff --cached | grep -i "api_key\|secret\|password\|token"

# ⚠️  IMPORTANT:
# The .gitignore file already protects all sensitive files.
# This file is just documentation for reference.
# 
# If you accidentally commit a secret:
# 1. ROTATE the secret immediately (generate new keys)
# 2. Remove from git history (git filter-branch or BFG)
# 3. Force push to remote repository
